%[text] %[text:anchor:T_915D5C47] # Intro to MATLAB® for Biological Sciences: Statistical Methods for Biology
%[text] In this module, we will learn how to use statistical methods in MATLAB to analyze ecologically relevant data. We will explore the data using **descriptive statistics**, **fit the data** using a predictive model, find the **linear correlations** between variables, and finally, discuss how you can use MATLAB to **test a hypothesis**. This module assumes basic MATLAB knowledge and it is recommended that all students take the [MATLAB Onramp](https://matlabacademy.mathworks.com/details/matlab-onramp/gettingstarted) before continuing if they have not already. 
%[text:tableOfContents]{"heading":"Table of Contents"}
%[text] 
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![](text:image:131b)
%[text]{"align":"center"} A flock of Chinstrap penguins in [Antarctica](https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Penguin_flock_in_Antarctica_(Unsplash).jpg). 
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_902E6DB5] ## Introduction
%[text] This module will use the [palmerpenguins](https://allisonhorst.github.io/palmerpenguins/) dataset from the [Biosciences: Working with Data](https://www.mathworks.com/matlabcentral/fileexchange/181586-biosciences-working-with-data) module \[[1](internal:M_6DCD441C), [2](internal:M_E8EC2DFF)\]. Refer back to that module for an introduction to the data. 
%[text] Statistical methods can help us (and everybody else!) understand the data we have collected. They help us to describe our findings qualitatively, draw conclusions from large and complex datasets, and explain how relevant and awesome these findings are to other biologists. Here, we will start by using MATLAB to describe and visualize some key points about our data. We'll then describe the relationship between variables in the data set by looking at what type of model might best describe that relationship. We can use this model to make predictions about the data. For example, if we describe the relationship between a penguin's body mass and flipper length, and then someone hands us the body mass of a new penguin, we can predict what its flipper length might be! We will also explore how 'in sync' or correlated different variables are, which gives us further information about their relationship. 
%[text] When we first start in biology, we learn that the [scientific method](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scientific_method) dictates that we should come up with some hypothesis and then collect data to test that hypothesis. Say you've done that and now you're staring at a giant pile of data. How do you know if that data supports or rejects your hypothesis? Luckily, it's straightforward to use MATLAB to test how our data supports or disproves a hypothesis! You've probably come across phrases like 'statistically significant' in scientific papers. Whether or not a result is statistically significant is determined by such hypothesis testing, which we will explore in the final part of this module. 
